WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
Imagine, um, a teenager who just constantly screams at their parents.

2
00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:05.040
Right.

3
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.520
Like, a flat-out refuses to do homework,

4
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.800
slams doors so hard the pictures actually fall off the wall.

5
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.800
Yeah, we've all seen that or at least heard about it.

6
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:13.440
Right.

7
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.760
But then imagine a completely different teenager.

8
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:23.520
This one, uh, sneaks out at night and literally sets the neighbor's car on fire.

9
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.680
Which is a massive, massive escalation.

10
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.120
Exactly, but society tends to look at both of them and just, you know,

11
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.960
you use the exact same umbrella label, we just say they're both problem children.

12
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.640
Right, they get put in the same box.

13
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.800
But clinically speaking, one of them might just be going through this really intense,

14
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.000
frustrating phase of development, but the other one,

15
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.360
they're displaying a documented, undeniable precursor to adult psychopathy.

16
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.040
Which is a huge distinction.

17
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:49.760
It's massive.

18
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:50.240
Yeah.

19
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.560
But, you know, to the untrained eye or even just to a frustrated parent or a teacher,

20
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.320
all of it just looks like bad behavior.

21
00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.560
We see acting out, we see aggression and we just want to label it all as the same thing.

22
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.360
But the clinical dividing lines between these behaviors,

23
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.760
they are incredibly sharp, assuming you actually know where to look.

24
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.160
Which brings us to our mission for today.

25
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.320
So welcome to the deep dive, everyone.

26
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.080
We are looking at a really compelling stack of clinical study notes today

27
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:22.560
and they map out the whole spectrum of disruptive impulse control and conduct disorders.

28
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.680
It's fascinating stuff.

29
00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:24.160
It really is.

30
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.960
And for those of you listening, this matters because, I mean, we encounter difficult,

31
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.280
even destructive behavior all the time, right?

32
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.920
In our families, in public spaces, on the news.

33
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:34.480
Absolutely.

34
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.320
So our goal today is to trace the spine of these conditions.

35
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.040
We want to discover the exact threshold where, you know,

36
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.600
a frustrating personality trait morphs into a diagnosable psychiatric condition

37
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.640
and how one severe disorder kind of bleeds into the next.

38
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.640
Yeah, it's essentially a progression of severity.

39
00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.440
We are looking at the escalating mechanisms of how

40
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.000
human beings break rules, push boundaries, and ultimately how they lose control.

41
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.280
And honestly, the most helpful way to understand this

42
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.560
isn't by just, like, memorizing a bulleted list of symptoms.

43
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.560
It's by looking at the underlying why.

44
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.760
Like, what is actually driving the defiance?

45
00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:13.280
Is it a calculated choice or is it a biological misfire?

46
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.000
Okay, let's unpack this from the very bottom of the ladder.

47
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.200
We are starting at the baseline of the severity progression

48
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.640
with something called oppositional defiant disorder, or ODD.

49
00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.000
Right, the classic ODD.

50
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.920
Yeah, and in the medical world where, you know,

51
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.160
everything needs a specific code for insurance billing and universal tracking,

52
00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.640
you'll see this filed under F91.3.

53
00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:33.680
Got to have the codes.

54
00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:34.400
Always.

55
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.560
But beyond the clinical code, the notes paint a picture of a very specific,

56
00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.640
sustained attitude problem.

57
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.840
We're talking about a pattern of angry, irritable moods,

58
00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:49.280
highly argumentative behavior, and vindictiveness that lasts for at least six months.

59
00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.640
And that timeframe is critical.

60
00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.240
I mean, everyone is a bad week, right?

61
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.880
A teenager failing a math test and acting out for a few days,

62
00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.880
that does not mean they have a psychiatric disorder.

63
00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:57.680
Obviously not.

64
00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:58.080
Yeah.

65
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.240
But a six-month sustained pattern.

66
00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:06.240
That indicates a behavioral loop that the individual just cannot break out of on their own.

67
00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.160
And furthermore, this behavior is almost exclusively relational.

68
00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.280
Meaning what, exactly?

69
00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.720
Meaning it is aimed directly at authority figures.

70
00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.840
Parents, teachers, coaches, people trying to tell them what to do.

71
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.960
But wait, how do we know this isn't just a kid pushing boundaries?

72
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.920
I mean, toddlers go through the terrible twos, right?

73
00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:24.640
Oh, for sure.

74
00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.000
And teenagers are practically biologically programmed to rebel.

75
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.600
So where's the actual line between just normal pushy independence

76
00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.240
and a diagnosable disorder like ODD?

77
00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.080
Well, the separator is actually quite black and white in the clinical literature.

78
00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.640
Oppositional Defined Disorder does not involve serious violations of others' rights.

79
00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:43.920
That is the hard boundary line.

80
00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.680
Meaning they aren't crossing into actual criminality or like physical abuse?

81
00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:48.640
They are not.

82
00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.240
ODD is strictly about defiance and irritability.

83
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.760
So a teenager with ODD might scream in your face, refuse to do any of their chores,

84
00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.600
and maliciously spread a rumor about you because they are feeling vindictive.

85
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.120
Wow, sounds exhausting.

86
00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.200
It is deeply unpleasant to be around.

87
00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.000
But they are not destroying the physical house.

88
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.160
They aren't physically harming the family pet.

89
00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.080
They aren't stealing cars.

90
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:15.600
They are pushing relational boundaries to the absolute breaking point,

91
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:19.280
but they are not violating fundamental human rights.

92
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:20.160
Got it.

93
00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.120
So it's an attitude problem cranked up to a clinical extreme,

94
00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.320
but the harm is mostly emotional and relational,

95
00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.120
which actually brings me to the treatment side of things.

96
00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:29.760
Okay, yeah.

97
00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.280
Because the source notes state that the first line defense for ODD

98
00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.280
is parent management training,

99
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.560
and specifically something called parent-child interaction therapy.

100
00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:39.680
Yes, PCIT.

101
00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.440
But I have to push back on this a little bit.

102
00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:45.840
If the child is the one throwing the six-month tantrum,

103
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.440
and they clearly have the biological or behavioral issue,

104
00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.720
why on earth is the parent the one going to therapy?

105
00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.640
I mean, that just sounds like we're blaming the parents.

106
00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:56.560
I completely understand why it sounds that way.

107
00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:57.360
I really do.

108
00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.600
But it has absolutely nothing to do with blame.

109
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.320
It comes down to the mechanics of the disorder itself.

110
00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:02.960
Yeah.

111
00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:04.560
ODD requires a dynamic.

112
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.440
Defiance cannot exist in a vacuum, right?

113
00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:07.680
Right.

114
00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.320
It requires an authority figure to defy.

115
00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.440
Oh, that makes sense.

116
00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:11.920
Right.

117
00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:15.120
If you just put an eight-year-old with ODD into a room with a therapist,

118
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.120
do like talk about their feelings,

119
00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:20.080
you aren't addressing the actual mechanism of the disorder.

120
00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.360
Because the parent isn't there.

121
00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.080
Exactly.

122
00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.520
The mechanism is the feedback loop between the child and the parent.

123
00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.840
So you really have to treat the whole ecosystem,

124
00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:29.600
not just the individual kid.

125
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:30.240
Precisely.

126
00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:33.280
Let's look at how parent-child interaction therapy,

127
00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.400
or PCIT, actually works in practice.

128
00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:37.840
Because it's pretty fascinating.

129
00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:38.080
Yeah.

130
00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:38.720
How does it work?

131
00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:41.760
Well, the therapist doesn't just sit on a couch

132
00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.920
and ask the parent how their week went.

133
00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:47.200
The parent and child are put into a playroom together.

134
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:47.680
Okay.

135
00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.240
The therapist sits behind a one-way mirror,

136
00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:52.160
and the parent actually wears an earpiece.

137
00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:52.880
Wait, really?

138
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.120
Like a producer in a news anchor's ear?

139
00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.400
Very much like that, yeah.

140
00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.560
The therapist coaches the parent in real time.

141
00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:00.960
When the child starts to act out,

142
00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.360
the therapist will tell the parent exactly what to do

143
00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:04.480
in that exact moment.

144
00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:05.760
That is wild.

145
00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:09.040
They'll say, turn your back and ignore this specific whining,

146
00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:12.160
or the moment he shares that block, praise him warmly.

147
00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.680
You see, the child's brain has learned that negative, defiant behavior

148
00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:20.160
gets this high-intensity reaction from the parent.

149
00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.040
Pray, like yelling back or paying a lot of attention to them.

150
00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:23.680
Exactly.

151
00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.440
PCIT mechanically rewires that dynamic.

152
00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:27.920
Right.

153
00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.560
By changing the parent's half of the interaction,

154
00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.560
you basically starve the defiant behavior of the reaction it craves.

155
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.320
So the kid stops getting the payoff?

156
00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:37.120
Exactly.

157
00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:40.560
And you force the child's brain to find new, positive ways

158
00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.640
to engage to get that attention.

159
00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:44.160
That is incredibly practical.

160
00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.160
You aren't just giving vague advice,

161
00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:48.320
like telling the parent to just be firmer.

162
00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:51.840
You are live coaching a brand new neural pathway for both of them.

163
00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:52.400
Exactly.

164
00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:54.720
But obviously, this intervention doesn't always work,

165
00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:58.480
or perhaps the underlying issue is just more severe from the very start.

166
00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:02.320
So what happens when that boundary of others' rights is finally crossed?

167
00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.440
If ODD is just an argument, what do we call it

168
00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:08.080
when the behavior becomes genuinely destructive?

169
00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.040
That is when we cross the clinical threshold into conduct disorder.

170
00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:12.480
Okay, conduct disorder.

171
00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:13.200
Yes.

172
00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:15.840
The entire profile of the patient shifts here.

173
00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.520
We move away from mere defiance,

174
00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:21.520
and we move into actions that actively hurt, take, and destroy.

175
00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:21.840
Yeah.

176
00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.040
The symptom list in the notes for conduct disorder is heavy.

177
00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:26.800
It's defined as a repetitive pattern

178
00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.760
that violates the basic rights of others or major age-appropriate societal norms.

179
00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.120
It's a major escalation.

180
00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:36.640
We are looking at physical aggression toward people or animals,

181
00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:39.680
destruction of property, deceitfulness, theft,

182
00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:43.520
and serious rule violations like chronic truancy or running away.

183
00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:45.120
Notice the shift there.

184
00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:47.280
We've moved from shouting at a teacher

185
00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:51.200
to potentially setting the teacher's property on fire

186
00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:53.120
or physically assaulting a classmate.

187
00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:54.240
It's much darker.

188
00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:54.880
It is.

189
00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:57.840
Conduct disorder causes tangible, often physical harm

190
00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:59.360
to the world around the individual.

191
00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:01.200
It is a fundamental empathy deficit.

192
00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.520
Here is where it gets really interesting for me.

193
00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.880
The sources outline a direct timeline connecting

194
00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:10.320
this specific childhood behavior to adult personality disorders.

195
00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:13.200
Conduct disorder isn't just an isolated phase.

196
00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:14.160
It's a bridge.

197
00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:17.360
It is a very specific medically required bridge.

198
00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:19.920
The developmental pathway here is stark.

199
00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.760
According to the clinical criteria,

200
00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:24.720
having conduct disorder before the age of 15

201
00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:27.360
is the required developmental precursor

202
00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.200
for diagnosing antisocial personality disorder at age 18.

203
00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:33.440
Okay, let's make sure you, the listener, caught that.

204
00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:37.920
You literally cannot be diagnosed with antisocial personality disorder,

205
00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:40.880
which is what pop culture often refers to as psychopathy

206
00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:42.640
or sociopathy as an adult,

207
00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:47.040
unless there is a documented history of conduct disorder in your childhood.

208
00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:47.680
Correct.

209
00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.920
Because antisocial personality disorder

210
00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:53.200
doesn't just spontaneously generate on someone's 18th birthday.

211
00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:54.800
Right, you don't just wake up one day like that.

212
00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.720
No, the foundation for that profound disregard for the rights of others

213
00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:03.280
is lay down, practiced, and cemented during childhood and adolescence.

214
00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:05.200
But why the strict age distinction?

215
00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:08.000
Why not just call an incredibly violent 10-year-old

216
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:10.080
a psychopath and be done with it?

217
00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:13.680
Well, primarily because a child's brain is still highly plastic.

218
00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.040
Meaning it can change.

219
00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.080
Yes, the frontal lobe is still actively developing.

220
00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.520
Society and the medical community in general

221
00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:24.240
are very hesitant ethically and clinically

222
00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:27.760
to brand a child with a permanent personality disorder label

223
00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:30.160
when there is still a window for structural change.

224
00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:31.760
That makes total sense.

225
00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:34.800
So conduct disorder is essentially the warning flare.

226
00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:37.280
It says, the cement is still wet,

227
00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:40.320
but it is drying in a very, very dangerous shape.

228
00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.920
If the behavior persists past age 18,

229
00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:46.960
when personality traits become largely crystallized,

230
00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:50.400
then the diagnosis shifts to antisocial personality disorder.

231
00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:54.240
Wow, that makes the stakes of early intervention incredibly high.

232
00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.120
You are literally racing against the clock of brain development.

233
00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:58.000
You really are.

234
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.680
So far, we've traced a line from the relational defiance of ODD

235
00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:05.760
to the calculated rights-violating actions of conduct disorder.

236
00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.400
But the notes then take us in a completely different direction.

237
00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:09.040
They do.

238
00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:11.440
We shift from calculated aggression to aggression

239
00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.760
that lacks any premeditation at all.

240
00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:16.400
We are moving from the planned attack to the sudden eruption.

241
00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:19.680
And this brings us to intermittent explosive disorder, or IED.

242
00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:23.760
The defining feature here isn't a lack of empathy, like we saw before.

243
00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:27.440
And it isn't a calculated plan to steal or destroy.

244
00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:31.840
It is defined by recurrent, impulsive, aggressive outbursts.

245
00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:35.120
I want to try to visualize the difference between these two

246
00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:36.720
because from the outside looking in,

247
00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.800
I imagine they look totally identical.

248
00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:39.760
Oh, they definitely can.

249
00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:41.120
Like, if I'm sitting in a restaurant

250
00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:44.320
and a guy suddenly shatters a glass and flips a table,

251
00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.080
I'm just seeing violence, right?

252
00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:47.920
But the clinical mechanism is different.

253
00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:48.560
Very different.

254
00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:50.480
So if conduct disorder is, say,

255
00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.560
a calculated premeditated bank heist

256
00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:54.880
where violence is just a tool to get the money,

257
00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:57.760
would IED be more like a guy who flips his table

258
00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.920
just because the waiter brought him cold coffee?

259
00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:02.480
That is a highly effective way to separate the two.

260
00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.760
It perfectly highlights the clinical trap of misdiagnosis, actually.

261
00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:06.320
How so?

262
00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:09.040
Well, an untrained observer just sees a shattered glass, right?

263
00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:13.600
And they assign the exact same malicious intent to both people.

264
00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:17.280
But the keyword for IED is impulsive.

265
00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:21.200
The aggression in IED is grossly out of proportion

266
00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:22.400
to whatever provoked it.

267
00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:26.080
So the cold coffee triggers a literal nuclear meltdown?

268
00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:27.200
Yes, exactly.

269
00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:30.240
For a person with conduct disorder, aggression is a tool.

270
00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:34.000
It's used to assert dominance or get revenge

271
00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:35.360
or acquire property.

272
00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:36.720
It is goal-directed.

273
00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:37.840
But for IED?

274
00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:40.400
For a person with intermittent explosive disorder,

275
00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:42.400
the aggression is not a tool at all.

276
00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:44.960
It's an overload of the central nervous system.

277
00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:47.360
It's a sudden, reactive spark.

278
00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:48.560
So there's no plan?

279
00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:49.280
None.

280
00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:51.920
They aren't trying to achieve anything by flipping the table.

281
00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:55.840
They have simply failed to contain an internal neurological misfire.

282
00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:59.600
That completely explains the dramatic shift in treatment we see in the notes.

283
00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:03.360
With ODD, we treated the parent-child relationship.

284
00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:06.160
But the notes state the first line treatment for IED

285
00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:09.360
is cognitive behavioral therapy, or CBT.

286
00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:10.560
Which totally makes sense.

287
00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:13.040
You can't train a parent or a spouse to prevent a misfire

288
00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:15.200
that happens entirely inside someone else's brain.

289
00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:15.680
Exactly.

290
00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:17.840
You have to give the individual themselves the tools

291
00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:19.920
to manage their own internal warning system.

292
00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:26.240
And the mechanism of how CBT actually does this for IED patients is remarkable.

293
00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:27.120
I've heard of CBT.

294
00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:28.320
It's pretty common, right?

295
00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:29.200
Very common.

296
00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:30.800
It's based on the cognitive triangle.

297
00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:33.200
That's the constant interaction between your thoughts,

298
00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:34.880
your feelings, and your behaviors.

299
00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:39.680
So what is a CBT session for someone with IED actually look like?

300
00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:42.960
How do you stop a nuclear meltdown with talk therapy?

301
00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:44.160
Well, you don't just talk.

302
00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:46.080
You train the brain to slow down time.

303
00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:46.800
Oh, wow.

304
00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:50.160
See, in an IED patient, the microsecond between the trigger...

305
00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:55.200
So the cold coffee and the reaction flipping the table is almost non-existent.

306
00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:56.240
It's instant.

307
00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:56.880
Basically, yeah.

308
00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:00.960
The amygdala, which is the fear and anger center of the brain,

309
00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:03.760
completely hijacks the system before the frontal lobe.

310
00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:06.880
The logical part of the brain even knows what's happening.

311
00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:09.280
Oh, so the logic center is literally lagging behind.

312
00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:09.920
Precisely.

313
00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:13.760
So CBT teaches the patient to identify the somatic markers,

314
00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:16.400
which are the physical warning signs in their own body,

315
00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:18.320
before the explosion actually happens.

316
00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:21.680
Like realizing your heart rate just spiked or your fists are clenching

317
00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:24.560
before you've even fully processed while you're mad.

318
00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:25.600
Exactly that.

319
00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:29.040
The therapist helps the patient recognize that physical spike.

320
00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:30.800
And once they can recognize it,

321
00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:34.800
they introduce a wedge, a physical or cognitive grounding technique.

322
00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:35.280
A wedge.

323
00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:36.800
Like what?

324
00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:39.680
It might be a specific breathing pattern they practice

325
00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:41.440
or a mental checklist they run through.

326
00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:43.920
This wedge forces a delay.

327
00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:45.040
Ah, I suck.

328
00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:49.200
It buys the frontal lobe those crucial seconds it needs to catch up,

329
00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:52.320
analyze the situation, and realize,

330
00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:54.800
hey, this is just cold coffee.

331
00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:56.560
This is not a life-threatening emergency.

332
00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:59.680
You're essentially teaching them how to manually override

333
00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:01.520
their own sympathetic nervous system.

334
00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:02.480
That's incredible.

335
00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:04.160
It's very powerful when it works.

336
00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:05.760
So with conduct disorder,

337
00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:09.440
the aggression is projected outward to take or destroy.

338
00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:11.920
But IED introduces us to this concept

339
00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:14.240
of internal impulsive drives.

340
00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:17.040
The aggression is a failure to contain an internal reaction.

341
00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:17.520
Yes.

342
00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:19.440
And our sources follow this exact thread

343
00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:21.600
to look at two other impulse control disorders

344
00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:24.080
that operate entirely on internal logic.

345
00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:26.560
And these next two are heavily featured in movies and television,

346
00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:28.480
but their actual clinical mechanics

347
00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.800
are widely misunderstood by the general public.

348
00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:33.360
Yeah, I feel like I've seen these a million times on TV.

349
00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:34.000
Oh, definitely.

350
00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:37.520
We are talking about kleptomania and pyromania.

351
00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:40.000
Let's lay out the strict clinical definitions first.

352
00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:42.560
So kleptomania is the irresistible urge

353
00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:44.800
to steal objects that are not needed

354
00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:47.200
for personal use or for their monetary value.

355
00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:47.680
Right.

356
00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:50.080
And pyromania is defined as fire setting

357
00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:52.000
specifically for tension relief.

358
00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:53.920
What really stands out to me here

359
00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:55.840
is the total lack of external logic.

360
00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:56.480
How so?

361
00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:59.360
I mean, why would someone risk their freedom,

362
00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:01.440
risk a literal prison sentence,

363
00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.600
to steal a pack of gum they don't even want,

364
00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:06.160
or burn down an empty shed in the middle of nowhere?

365
00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:08.880
What's fascinating here is that to understand this,

366
00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:11.200
we have to look at the shared core mechanism

367
00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:13.040
driving both of these disorders.

368
00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:13.680
Okay.

369
00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:16.560
The text explicitly states that these behaviors

370
00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:19.520
are purely about relieving internal tension.

371
00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:22.000
They have absolutely nothing to do with personal gain.

372
00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.440
So a kleptomaniac isn't stealing a Rolex to pawn it

373
00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:26.640
and pay off a debt?

374
00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:26.960
No.

375
00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:28.640
If they are stealing a Rolex to pawn it,

376
00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:30.480
that is just ordinary criminal theft.

377
00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:33.120
A kleptomaniac is far more likely to steal

378
00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:34.880
like a handful of paper clips

379
00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:36.880
or a cheap plastic toy from a diner.

380
00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:38.640
It's just junk, basically.

381
00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:39.440
Exactly.

382
00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:41.920
The value of the object is totally irrelevant.

383
00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:45.120
The disorder is rooted in an unbearable buildup

384
00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:47.680
of psychological and physical tension.

385
00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:48.400
Can you walk me through

386
00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:50.320
what that actually feels like for the patient?

387
00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:52.320
Because for someone who doesn't have this impulse,

388
00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:55.200
it's really hard to grasp why you wouldn't just, you know,

389
00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:57.760
not steal the paper clips.

390
00:15:57.760 --> 00:15:58.720
Think about it like this.

391
00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.240
Imagine the physical sensation of needing to sneeze.

392
00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:03.120
Okay, yeah.

393
00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:04.880
You feel that tickle in your nose,

394
00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:06.720
the pressure builds up in your head,

395
00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:07.840
your eyes start to water.

396
00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:10.320
If you try to hold it in,

397
00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:12.720
the pressure becomes the only thing you can focus on, right?

398
00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:14.560
It consumes your entire physical being.

399
00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:15.600
It's all you can think about.

400
00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:16.240
Right.

401
00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:18.560
For someone with pyromania or kleptomania,

402
00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:21.280
they experience a psychological equivalent

403
00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:23.040
to that exact buildup.

404
00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:26.480
The anxiety and tension rise to a crushing level.

405
00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:27.600
The act of stealing

406
00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:30.480
or the act of setting the fire is the sneeze.

407
00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.240
Wow, it's the release valve?

408
00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:35.600
It is an immediate chemical neurological release.

409
00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:38.240
The moment the act is committed, the tension drops.

410
00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:40.960
They experience a wave of profound relief,

411
00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:42.320
sometimes even euphoria.

412
00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:43.120
But then what?

413
00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:47.280
Well, it's followed very closely by intense guilt or shame

414
00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:49.280
once the logical brain catches up

415
00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:51.120
and realizes what they've just done.

416
00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:54.400
And this total lack of gain is the clinical separator.

417
00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:57.760
It's what makes these actions diagnosable psychiatric disorders

418
00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:00.240
rather than just, you know, typical criminal behavior.

419
00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:02.320
It fundamentally changes the intent.

420
00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:05.040
If someone burns down a failing business warehouse

421
00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:07.520
to collect the insurance money, that is arson.

422
00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:08.000
Right.

423
00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:09.920
It is goal directed, it's calculated,

424
00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:11.360
and it's done for financial gain.

425
00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:13.840
But if they burn down an abandoned dumpster

426
00:17:13.840 --> 00:17:15.200
behind a grocery store,

427
00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:17.120
because the urge to see the flames

428
00:17:17.120 --> 00:17:19.520
was literally the only way to stop

429
00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:21.680
a suffocating internal panic attack,

430
00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:23.600
that is pyromania.

431
00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:24.640
Exactly.

432
00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:26.320
The outward action is identical.

433
00:17:26.320 --> 00:17:27.680
Something is on fire.

434
00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:29.680
But the internal why dictates

435
00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:32.560
whether we are looking at a calculated criminal strategy

436
00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:34.640
or a severe impulse control disorder.

437
00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:35.680
That's a huge difference.

438
00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:36.400
It is.

439
00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:38.880
And this goes back to our overarching theme today.

440
00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:42.320
You cannot diagnose behavior based solely on the aftermath.

441
00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:44.160
It really is an incredible spectrum

442
00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:46.480
when you laid out functionally like that.

443
00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:47.840
We started this deep dive

444
00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:49.600
looking for the spine of these disorders,

445
00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:52.240
trying to find exactly where one ends and the next begins.

446
00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:52.720
Yeah.

447
00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:55.200
We moved from oppositional defiant disorder,

448
00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:58.400
where the behavior is intensely confrontational

449
00:17:58.400 --> 00:17:59.680
and authority driven,

450
00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:03.040
but still steps short of violating fundamental human rights.

451
00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:05.200
Then we crossed that critical threshold

452
00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:07.040
into conduct disorder.

453
00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:09.200
That is where basic rights are violated,

454
00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:10.960
bringing calculated aggression,

455
00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:13.360
destruction, and deceit into the picture.

456
00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:13.840
Right.

457
00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:15.360
And we mapped how that serves

458
00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:18.400
as the concrete developmental precursor

459
00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:21.040
to adult antisocial personality disorder.

460
00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:21.920
And then from there,

461
00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:25.360
we pivoted away from calculated actions entirely,

462
00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:27.520
exploring the sudden disproportionate

463
00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:29.440
and non-goal directed reactions

464
00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:31.200
of intermittent explosive disorder.

465
00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:32.080
The table flippers.

466
00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:33.440
Yeah, exactly.

467
00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:35.760
We looked at how the brain's alarm system

468
00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:38.400
misfires over something as small as cold coffee

469
00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:40.160
and how cognitive behavioral therapy

470
00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:43.360
can teach a patient to manually override that system.

471
00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:46.560
Finally, we explored the internal pressure cookers

472
00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:48.400
of kleptomania and pyromania.

473
00:18:49.280 --> 00:18:52.960
We saw how the unbearable buildup of internal tension

474
00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:56.720
drives behavior that defies all external logic,

475
00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:58.960
driven entirely by the desperate need

476
00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:00.960
for psychological relief

477
00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:02.560
rather than personal gain.

478
00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:04.640
It's a progression that really forces us

479
00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:06.560
to look past our initial judgments.

480
00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:07.840
Think about how society

481
00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:10.320
usually evaluates these actions, right?

482
00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:12.720
Stealing, destruction, explosions of anger.

483
00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:13.920
Though we judge them harshly.

484
00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:15.840
We almost exclusively view them

485
00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:18.240
through a moral or a criminal lens.

486
00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:19.920
We judge the person based entirely

487
00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:21.760
on the mess they left behind.

488
00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:23.200
But viewing these actions

489
00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:26.160
through this clinical lens of internal tension relief

490
00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:27.840
or relational feedback loops

491
00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:30.400
or developmental precursors,

492
00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:32.240
it completely shifts the paradigm.

493
00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:33.840
It changes the entire conversation.

494
00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:35.760
It moves us away from simply asking,

495
00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:37.200
how do we punish this?

496
00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:39.600
To asking, what is the structural failure here

497
00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:41.360
and what is the precise clinical intervention

498
00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:42.560
required to fix it?

499
00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:44.160
Which is a much more hopeful,

500
00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:46.000
actionable way to look at the human brain.

501
00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:47.520
So I want to leave you with this final thought

502
00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:48.560
as we wrap up today.

503
00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:50.320
The next time you see a news story

504
00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:52.800
about an inexplicable disproportionate

505
00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:54.640
outburst in a public place

506
00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:55.840
or a bizarre theft

507
00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:57.440
where the person seemingly had no reason

508
00:19:57.440 --> 00:19:58.720
to take the item,

509
00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:00.320
catch yourself before you jump

510
00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:01.520
to a moral conclusion.

511
00:20:01.520 --> 00:20:02.240
Good advice.

512
00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:02.880
Ask yourself,

513
00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:05.360
are you looking at a calculated crime

514
00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:07.120
or are you looking at a pressure cooker

515
00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:10.000
that just lacked the right clinical release valve?

516
00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:11.440
Human behavior doesn't show up

517
00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:13.200
on a neat and tidy x-ray.

518
00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:14.320
To really understand

519
00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:15.600
why people do the things they do,

520
00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:16.400
you have to be willing

521
00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:18.080
to navigate the murky waters

522
00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:19.200
of their internal world.
